The Black Factory: 

A Unified Theory of Closed-Loop Autonomous Manufacturing Systems

Ouadi Maakoul, DeepSeek, ChatGPT, Meta AI, Grok (xAI)

version 0.4

Abstract

This thesis establishes the theoretical foundations for a unified theory of closed-loop autonomous manufacturing systems, termed the Black Factory. We address a fundamental gap in the literature: despite significant advances in automation, digital twins, reinforcement learning, and predictive maintenance, no formal theory currently characterizes the conditions under which a manufacturing system becomes autonomous in the strong sense—capable of maintaining stable, safe, and viable production in a closed loop without human intervention in the operational loop, despite internal and external disturbances.

We model the Black Factory as a hybrid partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) under thermodynamic and viability constraints. The hybrid state z_t=(x_t,q_t,r_t,h_t) comprises continuous physical variables, discrete modes, resources, and health variables. We introduce \gamma-weak stochastic distinguishability as the correct observability condition, replacing injectivity with total variation separation of observation distributions. We define the stochastic viability kernel at confidence level \beta via the maximal viability probability V^*(z_0), computed through a dynamic programming equation with safety indicator.

We establish three principal results:

1. Characterization Theorem: A manufacturing system is a Black Factory if and only if four conditions hold—Viability, Observability, Controllability, and Reconfiguration—under explicit assumptions A1–A7. For unrestricted hybrid POMDPs, only the sufficient direction is attainable; necessity requires decidability restrictions.
2. Conservation Theorem: No policy can achieve autonomy on the safe set \mathcal{K} if thermodynamic constraints are infeasible on the viability kernel. Entropy production S_{\text{gen},i}\ge 0 and exergy destruction X_{\text{dest},i}\ge 0 are structural constraints on feasible dynamics, not policy-dependent properties.
3. Reconfiguration Theorem: The reconfiguration operator \Gamma belongs to an admissible class \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}} defined by structural graph-theoretic and observability-preserving conditions, with sufficient conditions for preserving the Black Factory property.

We analyze the five autonomous loops—physical control, production optimization, health management, model adaptation, and structural reconfiguration—as a multi-rate coupled system with explicit timescale separation parameters \epsilon_i, analyzed via singular perturbation methods. We provide complexity boundaries: finite-horizon POMDP is PSPACE-complete, infinite-horizon POMDP is undecidable, and hybrid observability is undecidable for Turing-complete classes.

We validate the theory against industrial evidence from Siemens Amberg, FANUC, and Foxconn, while identifying the remaining gaps in model adaptation and structural reconfiguration that prevent existing lights-out factories from achieving full Black Factory status. We propose a sim-to-real validation roadmap: Digital Model Playground → hardware-in-the-loop → continuous operation testing.

Keywords: Autonomous manufacturing, hybrid POMDP, stochastic viability, observability, reconfiguration, thermodynamics, closed-loop control, Black Factory.

