Chapter 1: Introduction and Problem Statement

1.1 Context

Over the past two decades, industrial and academic research has produced a set of technological and conceptual building blocks that, taken in isolation, represent major advances: advanced automation, digital twins, semantic interoperability, predictive maintenance, reconfigurable systems, and reinforcement learning. Yet no unified theory currently exists to formally characterize the conditions under which a manufacturing system becomes autonomous in the strong sense—that is, capable of maintaining stable, safe, and optimal production in a closed loop, without human intervention in the operational loop, despite internal and external disturbances.

A recent systematic review of 196 studies using reinforcement learning for production planning and control (Mayerhoff & Schmidt, 2026) establishes a decisive observation: none of the studied cases reports a durable, continuous autonomous control loop in a real industrial installation. Validation remains overwhelmingly simulation-based. This gap between algorithmic abundance and the absence of closed-loop deployment constitutes the starting point of our work.

1.2 Fundamental Question

We pose the following question:

What are the structural conditions under which a manufacturing system can maintain autonomous production in a closed loop despite disturbances, failures, demand variations, and internal state changes?

This is not a question of engineering in the narrow sense. It is theoretical in nature: it requires the definition of a mathematical object, the identification of its structural properties, and the derivation of measurable criteria for autonomy.

We deliberately avoid claiming necessary and sufficient conditions for arbitrary manufacturing systems. For general hybrid partially observable systems, finding an optimal policy for a finite-horizon POMDP is PSPACE-complete, and for an infinite horizon it is undecidable. Observability of piecewise-affine hybrid systems is likewise undecidable, because many classes of hybrid systems simulate Turing machines. We therefore proceed as follows:

· We derive structural necessary conditions that any autonomous factory must satisfy.
· We derive sufficient conditions under explicit restrictions on the system class, policy class, and information structure.
· We identify the boundary between decidable and undecidable regimes.

1.3 Preliminary Definition

We define the Black Factory not by the absence of humans—a negative and non-physical definition—but by a structural property:

\boxed{
\textbf{Black Factory}
=
\text{a manufacturing system whose operational state, decision process, physical actions, maintenance state, and structural configuration are autonomous in a closed loop.}
}

The term "black" denotes the operationally opaque character of the system to an external observer: its internal functioning becomes an autonomous black box whose behavior we must be able to characterize from the outside. This is precisely the object of a theory.

We distinguish three human roles:

· Human in the loop: Human is part of the control loop, making decisions or taking actions.
· Human on the loop: Human supervises and can intervene, but is not part of the operational loop.
· Human out of the operational loop: Human has no operational role; the system is fully autonomous.

The Black Factory is human-out-of-the-operational-loop, but not necessarily human-out-of-governance.

1.4 Thesis Structure

Chapter 2 critically reviews the building blocks. Chapter 3 formalizes the hybrid POMDP framework. Chapter 4 develops observability and viability theory. Chapter 5 presents the multi-rate loop architecture. Chapter 6 defines autonomy metrics. Chapter 7 states the characterization theorem. Chapter 8 establishes thermodynamic constraints. Chapter 9 develops reconfiguration theory. Chapter 10 analyzes decidability boundaries. Chapter 11 validates against industrial evidence and proposes a sim-to-real roadmap. Chapter 12 concludes.

