Chapter 2: Critical Review of Existing Building Blocks

We distinguish eight families of relevant contributions. For each, we identify its value for the theory, its limitations, and how it must be integrated or surpassed.

2.1 Architecture and Functional Decomposition

NIST proposes a functional architecture for smart manufacturing that decomposes production functions and information flows between components (NIST, 2016). A notable evolution consists in moving from a rigid hierarchical architecture to a network of cooperating specialized components.

Contribution. This vision justifies representing the factory as a graph G=(V,E) where V is the set of nodes (machines, cells, controllers, resources) and E the set of flows (material, energy, information).

Limitation. The architecture remains descriptive. It provides neither the conditions for autonomy nor the criteria for stability or viability.

2.2 Digital Twin and Digital Thread

NIST defines the digital twin as an artifact enabling observation, diagnosis, prediction, and optimization in near real time. ISO 23247 formalizes this framework: general principles (part 1), reference architecture (part 2), information exchanges (part 4). Part 5 (2026) introduces the digital thread, linking digital twins across the product lifecycle.

Contribution. The digital twin provides synchronization infrastructure between the physical world and its estimation: \text{Physical Factory}\leftrightarrow\text{Digital Twin}.

Limitation. The digital twin is an instrument of representation, not a principle of decision.

2.3 Semantic Interoperability

OPC UA provides an information model, message model, communication model, and conformance framework enabling heterogeneous industrial systems to communicate.

Contribution. Interoperability is a precondition for distributed observability.

Critical remark. The question is not only how machines communicate, but what minimal information a machine must expose for an autonomous factory to reason about it. We formalize this via stochastic distinguishability rather than injectivity.

For each node v_i\in V, define a projection \pi_i:\mathcal{Z}\to\mathcal{Y}_i. Two states z,z'\in\mathcal{Z} are stochastically distinguishable if:

\|O(\cdot\mid z)-O(\cdot\mid z')\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\ge\gamma>0.

The system is \gamma-weakly observable on the safe set \mathcal{K} if every pair of distinct states in \mathcal{K} is \gamma-stochastically distinguishable. This is the correct necessary condition for autonomy—strictly weaker than injectivity, and well-defined for stochastic systems.

2.4 Reconfigurable Manufacturing Systems

The literature on Reconfigurable Manufacturing Systems (RMS) studies how to design systems capable of changing their configuration in response to demand variations or production conditions (Koren et al., 1999). The six characteristics are modularity, integrability, diagnosability, convertibility, customization, and scalability.

Contribution. We reformulate the problem. Let G_t=(V_t,E_t) be the physical graph at time t. When a machine disappears (v_k\notin V_{t+1}), the system seeks:

\boxed{
G_{t+1}^{*}=\arg\max_{G}J(G)
}

under the new constraints. This gives Autonomous Topological Reconfiguration.

2.5 Petri Nets

Manufacturing systems are discrete-event dynamic systems. Petri nets represent concurrency, resources, synchronization, queues, machines, failures, repairs, and scheduling. Luo et al. (2024) combine Petri Net + GNN + Deep RL for AGV routing and task allocation.

Contribution. Petri nets provide a rigorous mathematical language for discrete-event dynamics.

Critical remark. Petri nets combinatorially explode with the number of places and transitions. The literature explicitly addresses "the well-known combinatorial explosion issue" in Timed Petri Nets. Hierarchical abstraction or reduction methods based on graph structure are necessary.

2.6 Reinforcement Learning

A 2026 systematic review of 196 studies on RL for production planning and control (Mayerhoff & Schmidt, 2026) notes a strong concentration on dispatching and a rise in multi-agent RL, but validation remains largely simulation-based. "The sim-to-real transfer gap continue[s] to hinder the widespread industrial application of RL."

Contribution. RL is a decision-making building block, not a complete theory of the factory.

Critical remark. \text{RL Factory}\neq\text{Autonomous Factory}.

2.7 Model Predictive Control and Digital Twin

MPC does something mathematically clean. At each instant x_t is observed, we predict x_{t+1},\ldots,x_{t+H} and solve:

\boxed{
u_{t:t+H}^{*}=\arg\min J
}

under physical constraints, applying only u_t^{*}.

Contribution. This gives the chain:

\boxed{
\text{AI}\rightarrow\text{Model}\rightarrow\text{Prediction}\rightarrow\text{Optimization}\rightarrow\text{Control}
}

2.8 Predictive Maintenance

The digital twin can represent the health state of each machine. We seek P(F_i\mid Y_{0:t}), then:

\mathrm{RUL}_i=\mathbb{E}\left[T_i^{\text{failure}}-t\mid Y_{0:t}\right]

Contribution.

\boxed{
\text{Factory State}=\text{Production State}+\text{Health State}
}

2.9 Industrial Proof: Lights-Out Factories

Siemens Amberg. "Production functions here on a largely automated basis, with 75 percent of the value chain handled independently by machines and robots." Quality is "99.9988 percent," translating to "roughly 12 defects per million."

FANUC. "By 2003, FANUC's robot-filled factory at the base of Mount Fuji was spawning 50 clones each day and could operate autonomously for up to a month."

Foxconn. "The site achieved levels of automation superior to 95% and operates in a model known as lights-out factory." "By implementing 40+ 4IR use cases, the site improved labour productivity by 190%, achieved 99.5% on-time delivery and cut manufacturing costs by 45%."

These cases confirm:

\boxed{
\text{Lights-out is not merely a futuristic vision.}
}

But they remain partially open-loop at the levels of model adaptation and structural reconfiguration.

