Chapter 3: Formalization — The Hybrid POMDP Framework

3.1 The Core Dynamical System

We model the Black Factory as a hybrid partially observable Markov decision process under thermodynamic and viability constraints.

Let the hybrid state be:

z_t=(x_t,q_t,r_t,h_t)

where:

· x_t\in\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_x}: continuous physical variables;
· q_t\in\mathcal{Q}: discrete modes (including topology and configuration);
· r_t\in\mathcal{R}: resources;
· h_t\in\mathcal{H}: health, wear, and latent degradation variables.

Let \xi_t\in\Xi denote the vector of model parameters (possibly unknown and time-varying).

The dynamics are:

z_{t+1}\sim F(z_t,u_t,w_t;\xi_t)

where u_t\in\mathcal{U}(z_t) is the admissible action, and w_t\in\mathcal{W} is the exogenous disturbance.

Observations are drawn from a conditional observation kernel:

y_t\sim O(\cdot\mid z_t)

This formulation is valid for hybrid states; an additive model y_t=H(z_t)+\varepsilon_t is only appropriate for continuous sub-states.

Belief state (including parameters):

b_t=P(z_t,\xi_t\mid y_{0:t},u_{0:t-1})

Policy:

u_t=\pi(b_t)

Topology is encoded in the discrete mode:

G_t=\mathcal{G}(q_t)

Remark. Topology G_t is a deterministic function of q_t, which is already contained in z_t. Therefore, G_t is redundant inside the belief state and the policy. We adopt the representation where topology is absorbed into q_t, and the policy depends only on the belief b_t. The explicit notation G_t=\mathcal{G}(q_t) is retained only where the graph structure is operationally relevant (e.g., in the reconfiguration operator \Gamma).

3.2 Thermodynamic Constraints

For each node i of the production graph, the mass, energy, entropy, and exergy balance laws must hold almost everywhere.

\frac{dm_i}{dt}=\sum_{\text{in}}\dot m-\sum_{\text{out}}\dot m-\dot m_{\text{waste}}

\frac{dE_i}{dt}=\sum_{\text{in}}\dot E-\sum_{\text{out}}\dot E+Q_i+W_i

\frac{dS_i}{dt}=\sum_{\text{in}}\dot S-\sum_{\text{out}}\dot S+\frac{Q_i}{T_i}+S_{\text{gen},i}

with:

\boxed{S_{\text{gen},i}\ge 0}

This is the second law. We also add exergy:

\frac{dX_i}{dt}=\sum_{\text{in}}\dot X-\sum_{\text{out}}\dot X-X_{\text{dest},i}

with:

\boxed{X_{\text{dest},i}\ge 0}

The exergy destruction X_{\text{dest}} is proportional to entropy production S_{\text{gen}}, so the two constraints are not independent. Information does not fabricate anything. It pilots a physical transformation that must respect mass, energy, entropy, and exergy.

3.3 The Physical Admissibility Set

The thermodynamic constraints define a physical admissibility set:

\mathcal{K}_{\text{physics}}=\left\{z\in\mathcal{Z}\;\middle|\;\exists u:\text{ balances hold and }S_{\text{gen},i}\ge0,\;X_{\text{dest},i}\ge0\right\}

The overall safe set is:

\mathcal{K}=K_{\text{safety}}\cap K_{\text{physics}}\cap K_{\text{quality}}

This merges thermodynamic feasibility with safety and quality constraints.

3.4 The Optimization Problem

The general problem is a constrained hybrid POMDP under thermodynamic and viability constraints:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\max_{\pi}\quad&
\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T}\gamma^t R(z_t,u_t)\right]\\
\text{s.t.}\quad&
z_{t+1}\sim F(z_t,u_t,w_t;\xi_t)\\
&
y_t\sim O(\cdot\mid z_t)\\
&
b_t=P(z_t,\xi_t\mid y_{0:t},u_{0:t-1})\\
&
u_t=\pi(b_t)\\
&
C(z_t,u_t)\le 0\\
&
z_t\in\mathcal{K}\quad\forall t\\
&
z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})\\
&
\dot m,\dot E,\dot S,\dot X\text{ compatible}\\
&
G_{t+1}=\Gamma(G_t,z_t,u_t)
\end{aligned}
}

Remark on the role of \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}). The viability kernel is not a state constraint at every t. It is a feasibility condition on the initial state: the policy must be chosen from the class of viable policies, and the initial state must belong to the \beta-viability kernel. The actual state constraint is z_t\in\mathcal{K} for all t, with viability emerging from the policy class. This avoids circularity.

Complexity caveat. For finite-horizon POMDPs, computing an optimal policy is PSPACE-complete (Papadimitriou & Tsitsiklis, 1987). For infinite-horizon POMDPs, the optimal policy existence problem is undecidable (Madani, Hanks & Condon, 2003). The hybrid POMDP considered here is at least as hard as the discrete POMDP.

