Chapter 4: Observability, Viability, and Stochastic Distinguishability

4.1 Stochastic Distinguishability

For the hybrid POMDP, the appropriate observability notion is stochastic distinguishability: two states z,z'\in\mathcal{K} are distinguishable if:

\|O(\cdot\mid z)-O(\cdot\mid z')\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\ge\gamma>0.

The system is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K} if every pair of distinct states in \mathcal{K} satisfies this condition. This is strictly weaker than injectivity of the observation map and is well-defined for stochastic systems.

4.2 The Stochastic Viability Kernel

Let \mathcal{K}\subseteq\mathcal{Z} be the closed safe set of admissible states. For stochastic systems, the probability-1 viability kernel is too restrictive. Following Doyen & De Lara (2010), we define the stochastic viability kernel at confidence level \beta as:

\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})
=
\left\{
z_0\in\mathcal{K}
\;\middle|\;
V^*(z_0)\ge\beta
\right\}

where the maximal viability probability is:

V^*(z_0)
=
\sup_{\pi}
\;
P\left(\forall t\ge 0,\; z_t\in\mathcal{K}\mid z_0,\pi\right).

Doyen & De Lara (2010) prove that V^* satisfies the dynamic programming equation:

V^*(z)=
\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{K}}(z)
\cdot
\max_{u\in\mathcal{U}(z)}
\mathbb{E}_{w}
\left[
V^*(F(z,u,w))
\right]

with boundary condition V^*(z)=0 for z\notin\mathcal{K}. The indicator \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{K}}(z) is essential: without it, V^* could be positive outside \mathcal{K} if dynamics return. The viability kernel at confidence level \beta is the section of level \beta of this value function:

z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})
\iff
V^*(z_0)\ge\beta.

4.3 The Fundamental Decomposition

The true property is:

\boxed{
\text{Black Factory}
=
\text{Viability}
+
\text{Observability}
+
\text{Controllability}
+
\text{Reconfiguration}
}

A factory that observes everything, controls everything, but leaves its safety domain is not autonomous. It is merely dangerous with good sensors.

4.4 Related Formal Frameworks

The Black Factory theory is positioned relative to:

· Supervisory Control Theory (SCT): Ramadge & Wonham's framework. Our stochastic distinguishability condition is a stochastic analogue of SCT observability. In SCT, observability means: "if an action must be disabled after a sequence, then this action must also be disabled after each sequence which is undistinguishable from it."
· Diagnosability: The ability to detect and isolate failures. Closely related to maintenance autonomy.
· Barrier Certificates: Control-theoretic tool for safety verification. "Neural Lyapunov and barrier certificates have recently been used as powerful tools for verifying the safety and stability properties of deep RL controllers." The condition \mathcal{L}_{f}B_{\epsilon}(x)<0 ensures the barrier function decreases.
· Constrained MDPs (cMDPs): Standard framework for RL with safety constraints.
· Stochastic Viability Theory: Doyen & De Lara (2010) provide the dynamic programming equation.

