Chapter 5: The Multi-Rate Loop Architecture

5.1 The Five Autonomous Loops

Instead of a single loop \text{Sense}\rightarrow\text{Think}\rightarrow\text{Act}, we propose five nested loops operating at distinct timescales:

Loop 1 — Physical control (milliseconds to seconds): X\rightarrow U\rightarrow X'

Loop 2 — Production optimization (minutes to hours): (Q,E,W,T)\rightarrow\text{Optimization}

Loop 3 — Health (hours to weeks): X_{\text{machine}}\rightarrow P(\text{Failure})\rightarrow\text{Maintenance}

Loop 4 — Model adaptation (days to months): \text{Data}\rightarrow\xi_{t+1}

Loop 5 — Structural reconfiguration (weeks to years): G_t\rightarrow G_{t+1}

5.2 Multi-Rate Coupled System

These loops do not compose sequentially. They form a multi-rate coupled system:

\boxed{
\mathcal{BF}
=
\left(
\mathcal{L}_P,
\mathcal{L}_O,
\mathcal{L}_H,
\mathcal{L}_M,
\mathcal{L}_R
\right)
}

5.3 Timescale Separation via Singular Perturbation

The most popular time-scale decomposition approach in control theory is the singular perturbation approach, which reduces model order by separating the time scales. We introduce explicit small parameters \epsilon_i>0 for i=1,\ldots,4 representing the ratio of timescales between adjacent loops:

\epsilon_1=\frac{\tau_P}{\tau_O},\quad
\epsilon_2=\frac{\tau_O}{\tau_H},\quad
\epsilon_3=\frac{\tau_H}{\tau_M},\quad
\epsilon_4=\frac{\tau_M}{\tau_R}

with \epsilon_i\in(0,\epsilon_{\max}) and \epsilon_{\max}\ll 1. Under these conditions, the coupled system admits a standard singular perturbation form, and the boundary-layer systems are exponentially stable uniformly in the slow variables. This allows the reduced-order approximation to be validated via Tikhonov's theorem and its discrete-time analogues.

The composition order must be derived from these timescale constraints, not assumed a priori.

