Chapter 6: Metrics of Autonomy

6.1 Vector-Valued Autonomy Profile

We do not fix the form of a scalar autonomy index \Psi a priori. We first define a vector-valued autonomy profile:

\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BF}}=(A_I,A_D,A_P,A_M,R_F)

where:

· Informational autonomy A_I: Quality of state estimation. Define:
  A_I = 1 - \frac{H(b_t)}{H_{\max}}
  or normalized observability Gramian eigenvalue.
· Decisional autonomy A_D: Performance under uncertainty:
  A_D = 1 - \frac{J(\pi^*)-J(\pi)}{J(\pi^*)}
  with oracle defined via digital twin as surrogate.
· Physical autonomy A_P: Degree of actuation and reconfiguration capability without human intervention.
· Maintenance autonomy A_M: Ability to predict, diagnose, and repair failures autonomously.
· Resilience R_F: Ratio of post-disturbance performance to nominal performance, extended to reconfiguration speed—time to return to \mathcal{K} after a failure. The POC (Performance Operating Characteristic) metric from CPPS resilience literature is "the most practically valuable quantitative resilience metric to assess the level of resilience in human-centered CPPS workstations in SMEs."

6.2 Aggregation

A global metric, if one is desired, takes the form A_{\mathrm{BF}}=\Psi(\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BF}}). The form of \Psi must be derived from explicit decision-theoretic axioms—Pareto dominance, risk sensitivity, or utility aggregation—not chosen for aesthetic reasons.

We propose aggregation as Liebig's law of the minimum:

A_{\mathrm{BF}} = \min_i A_i

because autonomy fails if any loop fails. Alternatively, aggregation via viability margin d(z,\partial\mathcal{K}).

