Chapter 7: The Characterization Theorem

7.1 Statement

Under assumptions A1–A7, a manufacturing system is a Black Factory if and only if the following four conditions hold:

1. Viability: z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}) for some \beta\in(0,1).
2. Observability: The system is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K} for some \gamma>0.
3. Controllability: All modes in \mathcal{Q} are reachable from the initial mode.
4. Reconfiguration: The reconfiguration operator \Gamma belongs to the admissible class \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}.

Caveat: For unrestricted hybrid POMDPs, only the sufficient direction is attainable. Necessity requires decidability restrictions (A7).

7.2 Assumptions

A1 (System class). The factory is modeled as a hybrid POMDP with finite discrete mode set \mathcal{Q}, compact continuous state space \mathcal{X}, and Lipschitz-continuous dynamics F on each mode.

A2 (Observation class). The observation kernel O(\cdot\mid z) admits a density with respect to a reference measure, and the induced belief update is well-defined and continuous in the weak topology. Furthermore, the system is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K} for some \gamma>0.

A3 (Safe set). The safe set \mathcal{K}\subseteq\mathcal{Z} is closed, and its stochastic viability kernel \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}) is non-empty for some confidence level \beta\in(0,1).

A4 (Thermodynamic feasibility). The dynamics F is admissible only if there exists a trajectory such that mass and energy balances hold with S_{\text{gen},i}\ge 0 and X_{\text{dest},i}\ge 0 on all nodes.

A5 (Timescale separation). The five loops admit a strict timescale hierarchy with explicit small parameters \epsilon_i\in(0,\epsilon_{\max}), so that the coupled system can be analyzed by singular perturbation methods.

A6 (Reconfiguration class). The reconfiguration operator \Gamma belongs to a class \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}} defined by structural conditions:

\Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}
\iff
\begin{cases}
\text{(i) }\Gamma\text{ preserves connectivity of material flow graph},\\
\text{(ii) }\Gamma\text{ preserves }\lambda_{\min}(W_o)\ge\epsilon\text{ (observability Gramian)},\\
\text{(iii) }\Gamma\text{ satisfies thermodynamic feasibility (A4)},\\
\text{(iv) }\Gamma\text{ satisfies safety constraints }C(z,u)\le 0.
\end{cases}

A7 (Decidability restriction). For the necessity direction, we restrict to hybrid systems whose observability problem is decidable—e.g., piecewise-affine systems with no simulation of Turing machines.

7.3 The Three Theorems

Theorem 1 (Characterization). A manufacturing system satisfying A1–A7 is a Black Factory if and only if conditions (1)–(4) hold.

Theorem 2 (Conservation). No policy can achieve autonomy on \mathcal{K} if the thermodynamic constraints are infeasible on \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}).

Theorem 3 (Reconfiguration). If \Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}, then \Gamma preserves the Black Factory property under A1–A7.

