Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Stochastic Viability Theory

The stochastic viability framework of Doyen & De Lara (2010) provides the mathematical foundation for the Black Factory viability kernel. We recall the essential definitions and the dynamic programming characterization.

Definition A.1 (Maximal Viability Probability). Let w(\cdot) denote a random process with marginal distribution \mathbb{P}, and let \mathcal{K}\subseteq\mathcal{Z} be a closed constraint set. The maximal viability probability is:

V^*(z_0) = \sup_{\pi} \mathbb{P}\left(w(\cdot)\in\Omega \;\middle|\; z_t\in\mathcal{K},\; t=t_0,\ldots,T \;\middle|\; z_0,\pi\right)

where \Omega is the set of admissible disturbance trajectories.

Proposition A.1 (Dynamic Programming Equation). The maximal viability probability satisfies the recursive equation:

V^*(z) = \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{K}}(z) \cdot \max_{u\in\mathcal{U}(z)} \mathbb{E}_w\left[V^*(F(z,u,w))\right]

with terminal condition V^*(z)=0 for z\notin\mathcal{K}. The proof is given in Doyen & De Lara (2010, Appendix A).

Definition A.2 (Stochastic Viability Kernel). The stochastic viability kernel at confidence level \beta is:

\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}) = \left\{z_0\in\mathcal{K} \;\middle|\; V^*(z_0)\ge\beta\right\}

Proposition A.2 (Monotonicity). For \beta_1\le\beta_2, \operatorname{Viab}_{\beta_2}(\mathcal{K})\subseteq\operatorname{Viab}_{\beta_1}(\mathcal{K}).

Proof. Immediate from the definition: if V^*(z_0)\ge\beta_2, then V^*(z_0)\ge\beta_1. \square

A.2 Stochastic Distinguishability

Definition A.3 (Total Variation Distance). For two probability measures \mu,\nu on a measurable space (\mathcal{Y},\mathcal{B}), the total variation distance is:

\|\mu-\nu\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \sup_{A\in\mathcal{B}} |\mu(A)-\nu(A)|

Definition A.4 (\gamma-Weak Observability). The hybrid POMDP is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K} if for all z,z'\in\mathcal{K} with z\neq z':

\|O(\cdot\mid z)-O(\cdot\mid z')\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\ge\gamma>0

Lemma A.1 (Relation to Injectivity). If the observation kernel O(\cdot\mid z) is injective on \mathcal{K}, then there exists \gamma>0 such that the system is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K}.

Proof. Injectivity implies O(\cdot\mid z)\neq O(\cdot\mid z') for all z\neq z' in \mathcal{K}. Since \mathcal{K} is compact and z\mapsto O(\cdot\mid z) is continuous in the weak topology (Assumption A2), the function (z,z')\mapsto\|O(\cdot\mid z)-O(\cdot\mid z')\|_{\mathrm{TV}} attains its minimum on the compact set \{(z,z')\in\mathcal{K}^2: z\neq z'\}. This minimum is strictly positive. \square

Remark A.1. The converse is false: \gamma-weak observability does not imply injectivity. Two distinct states can have distinct but overlapping observation distributions separated by \gamma.

A.3 Hybrid Systems and Observability

Definition A.5 (Piecewise-Affine Hybrid System). A hybrid system is piecewise-affine if on each discrete mode q\in\mathcal{Q}, the dynamics take the form:

\dot{x} = A_q x + B_q u + a_q, \quad y = C_q x + c_q

where A_q,B_q,C_q are matrices of appropriate dimensions and a_q,c_q are constant vectors.

Theorem A.1 (Collins & van Schuppen, 2004). Observability is undecidable for Turing machines with output. Since many classes of hybrid systems simulate Turing machines, observability for these classes is undecidable. For piecewise-affine hybrid systems, observability properties cannot be easily deduced from those of the component linear subsystems.

Remark A.2. This result motivates Assumption A7: for the necessity direction of the Characterization Theorem, we restrict to hybrid systems whose observability problem is decidable.

A.4 Barrier Certificates

Definition A.6 (Barrier Certificate). A differentiable function B:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R} is a barrier certificate for the safe set \mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{X} and unsafe set \mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathcal{X} if:

B(x)\le 0 \quad \forall x\in\mathcal{S}, \qquad B(x)>0 \quad \forall x\in\mathcal{U}

and

\mathcal{L}_f B(x) \le 0 \quad \forall x\in\mathcal{X}

where \mathcal{L}_f B is the Lie derivative of B along the vector field f.

Theorem A.2. If such a barrier certificate exists, then the safe set \mathcal{S} is forward invariant: if x(0)\in\mathcal{S}, then x(t)\in\mathcal{S} for all t\ge 0.

Remark A.3. Neural Lyapunov and barrier certificates have recently been used as powerful tools for verifying the safety and stability properties of deep reinforcement learning controllers. The key condition \mathcal{L}_{f(x,\mathcal{F}_u^\Psi)}B_{\epsilon}(x)<0 ensures the barrier function decreases along trajectories, keeping the system within the safe set.

