Appendix B: Proofs of the Main Theorems

B.1 Proof of the Characterization Theorem (Theorem 1)

Theorem 1 (Characterization). Under Assumptions A1–A7, a manufacturing system is a Black Factory if and only if the following four conditions hold:

1. Viability: z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}) for some \beta\in(0,1).
2. Observability: The system is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K} for some \gamma>0.
3. Controllability: All modes in \mathcal{Q} are reachable from the initial mode.
4. Reconfiguration: The reconfiguration operator \Gamma belongs to the admissible class \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}.

Proof (Sufficient Direction).

Assume conditions (1)–(4) hold. We show that the system is a Black Factory.

Step 1: Viability. By condition (1), z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}). By Proposition A.1, there exists a policy \pi such that:

\mathbb{P}\left(\forall t\ge 0,\; z_t\in\mathcal{K}\mid z_0,\pi\right) \ge \beta

Thus, the system can remain in the safe set with probability at least \beta.

Step 2: Observability. By condition (2), the system is \gamma-weakly observable on \mathcal{K}. By Lemma A.1, distinct states in \mathcal{K} are distinguishable from observations. Therefore, the belief state b_t=P(z_t,\xi_t\mid y_{0:t},u_{0:t-1}) is well-defined and the policy u_t=\pi(b_t) can be computed.

Step 3: Controllability. By condition (3), all modes in \mathcal{Q} are reachable. Therefore, the system can transition between any production configurations as needed.

Step 4: Reconfiguration. By condition (4), \Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}. By Assumption A6, \Gamma preserves viability, observability, thermodynamic feasibility, and safety. Therefore, the system can reconfigure its topology without leaving the Black Factory regime.

Combining Steps 1–4, the system satisfies all four properties of a Black Factory: it can remain viable, observe its state, control its actions, and reconfigure its structure. Therefore, it is a Black Factory. \square

Proof (Necessary Direction under A7).

Assume the system is a Black Factory. We show that conditions (1)–(4) must hold.

Step 1: Viability. If the system is a Black Factory, it maintains production in a closed loop despite disturbances. Therefore, there exists a policy \pi such that z_t\in\mathcal{K} for all t with probability at least \beta. By definition, z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}).

Step 2: Observability. If the system is a Black Factory, it estimates its latent state from observations. Therefore, distinct states must produce distinguishable observations. If the system were not \gamma-weakly observable, there would exist z\neq z' in \mathcal{K} with identical observation distributions, making state estimation impossible. This contradicts the autonomy requirement. Hence, \gamma-weak observability holds.

Step 3: Controllability. If the system is a Black Factory, it can execute actions to maintain production and reconfigure. Therefore, all modes must be reachable.

Step 4: Reconfiguration. If the system is a Black Factory, it can reconfigure its topology autonomously. By Assumption A6, this implies \Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}.

Under Assumption A7, the observability problem is decidable, so the necessity direction is attainable. \square

Remark B.1. For unrestricted hybrid POMDPs, only the sufficient direction is guaranteed, since observability is undecidable (Theorem A.1).

B.2 Proof of the Conservation Theorem (Theorem 2)

Theorem 2 (Conservation). No policy can achieve autonomy on \mathcal{K} if the thermodynamic constraints are infeasible on \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}).

Proof.

Assume \mathcal{K}_{\text{physics}}\cap\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})=\emptyset.

By definition, \mathcal{K}_{\text{physics}} is the set of states for which there exists an admissible action u such that mass and energy balances hold and S_{\text{gen},i}\ge 0, X_{\text{dest},i}\ge 0 for all nodes.

If \mathcal{K}_{\text{physics}}\cap\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})=\emptyset, then for every z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}), the thermodynamic constraints cannot be satisfied on the viability kernel.

By the second law of thermodynamics, S_{\text{gen},i}\ge 0 is a physical constraint that cannot be violated by any policy. If this constraint is infeasible on \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}), then no admissible trajectory can remain in \mathcal{K} indefinitely.

Therefore, the maximal viability probability V^*(z_0)=0 for all z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}). By definition, \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})=\{z_0: V^*(z_0)\ge\beta\}, which is empty if V^*=0. This contradicts the assumption that \operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})\neq\emptyset (Assumption A3).

Hence, no policy can achieve autonomy on \mathcal{K}. \square

Corollary B.1. If \mathcal{K}_{\text{physics}}\cap\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K})\neq\emptyset, then autonomy is thermodynamically feasible.

B.3 Proof of the Reconfiguration Theorem (Theorem 3)

Theorem 3 (Reconfiguration). If \Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}, then \Gamma preserves the Black Factory property under Assumptions A1–A7.

Proof.

Assume \Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}. By Assumption A6, \Gamma satisfies:

\Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}
\iff
\begin{cases}
\text{(i) }\Gamma\text{ preserves connectivity of material flow graph},\\
\text{(ii) }\Gamma\text{ preserves }\lambda_{\min}(W_o)\ge\epsilon\text{ (observability Gramian)},\\
\text{(iii) }\Gamma\text{ satisfies thermodynamic feasibility (A4)},\\
\text{(iv) }\Gamma\text{ satisfies safety constraints }C(z,u)\le 0.
\end{cases}

Step 1: Viability preservation. Condition (i) ensures that the material flow graph remains connected, so material and energy can flow between nodes. Condition (iii) ensures thermodynamic feasibility. Together, these ensure that the viability kernel is preserved: if z_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_\beta(\mathcal{K}), then after applying \Gamma, the system remains viable.

Step 2: Observability preservation. Condition (ii) ensures that the observability Gramian satisfies \lambda_{\min}(W_o)\ge\epsilon, so the system remains \gamma-weakly observable. Therefore, state estimation remains possible after reconfiguration.

Step 3: Controllability preservation. Condition (i) ensures connectivity, which implies mode reachability is preserved. Therefore, controllability is maintained.

Step 4: Safety preservation. Condition (iv) ensures that safety constraints remain satisfied.

By Theorem 1, the four conditions of the Characterization Theorem remain satisfied after applying \Gamma. Therefore, \Gamma preserves the Black Factory property. \square

Remark B.2. The observability Gramian condition \lambda_{\min}(W_o)\ge\epsilon is validated for LTI systems. For piecewise-affine hybrid systems, a geometric sufficient condition based on matrix rank exists, but a general algebraic test is not established. This remains an open problem.

