Appendix C: Complexity Proofs and Decidability Boundaries

C.1 POMDP Complexity

Theorem C.1 (Papadimitriou & Tsitsiklis, 1987). For finite-horizon POMDPs, computing an optimal policy is PSPACE-complete. All three variants—finite horizon, infinite horizon discounted, and infinite horizon average cost—are complete for P.

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds by reduction from the quantified Boolean formula (QBF) problem, which is PSPACE-complete. The POMDP is constructed such that an optimal policy encodes a satisfying assignment for the QBF. The key insight is that the partially observed state allows the policy to simulate the quantifier alternation in QBF. \square

Theorem C.2 (Madani, Hanks & Condon, 2003). For infinite-horizon POMDPs, the optimal policy existence problem is undecidable under discounted and undiscounted total reward models, average-reward models, and state-avoidance models.

Proof sketch. The proof reduces from the halting problem for two-counter machines (Minsky machines), which is undecidable. The POMDP is constructed such that the existence of an optimal policy corresponds to the halting of the machine. \square

Corollary C.1. The hybrid POMDP considered in this thesis is at least as hard as the discrete POMDP, since the discrete POMDP is a special case (single mode, no continuous dynamics). Therefore, the complexity lower bounds apply directly.

C.2 Hybrid Observability Undecidability

Theorem C.3 (Collins & van Schuppen, 2004). Observability is undecidable for Turing machines with output. Since many classes of hybrid systems simulate Turing machines, observability for these classes is undecidable.

Proof sketch. An elementary example is given showing that observability is undecidable for Turing machines with output. The proof constructs a Turing machine whose output sequence encodes the halting behavior. Since many hybrid systems can simulate Turing machines, observability is undecidable for these classes. \square

Corollary C.2. For piecewise-affine hybrid systems, observability properties cannot be easily deduced from those of the component linear subsystems.

C.3 Petri Net State Explosion

Theorem C.4. The reachability graph of a Petri net grows exponentially with the number of places and transitions.

Proof sketch. Each place can hold a non-negative integer number of tokens. If the net has n places with maximum capacity k, the state space has size (k+1)^n, which is exponential in n. For unbounded nets, the state space is infinite.

Remark C.1. State space construction is stalled by "the state explosion problem which makes it a tough task or even impossible when the state space computation demands prohibitive cost (time and memory)".

Theorem C.5 (Hierarchical Decomposition). Hierarchical abstraction mitigates state explosion without altering the theoretical foundation.

Proof sketch. By decomposing the Petri net into modules and computing the reachability graph of each module separately, the overall state space can be reconstructed via superposition. The complexity becomes polynomial in the number of modules rather than exponential in the total number of places.

