Appendix D: Details of the Five Autonomous Loops

D.1 Loop 1: Physical Control

Timescale: Milliseconds to seconds.

Dynamics: X\rightarrow U\rightarrow X'

Formalization: The physical control loop operates on the continuous state x_t\in\mathcal{X} with control input u_t\in\mathcal{U}(x_t). The dynamics are:

\dot{x} = f(x,u,w;\xi)

where f is Lipschitz-continuous on each mode (Assumption A1).

Control Objective: Maintain x_t\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{safety}} and track a reference trajectory x_{\text{ref}}(t).

D.2 Loop 2: Production Optimization

Timescale: Minutes to hours.

Dynamics: (Q,E,W,T)\rightarrow\text{Optimization}

Formalization: Given the production state (q_t,r_t), the production optimization loop solves:

\max_{u_{t:t+H}} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{k=0}^{H} R(z_{t+k},u_{t+k})\right]

subject to production constraints C(z,u)\le 0.

Connection to MPC: This loop implements the chain:

\text{AI}\rightarrow\text{Model}\rightarrow\text{Prediction}\rightarrow\text{Optimization}\rightarrow\text{Control}

D.3 Loop 3: Health Management

Timescale: Hours to weeks.

Dynamics: X_{\text{machine}}\rightarrow P(\text{Failure})\rightarrow\text{Maintenance}

Formalization: The health state h_t\in\mathcal{H} evolves according to degradation dynamics. The failure probability is:

P(F_i\mid Y_{0:t}) = \int P(F_i\mid h_t) P(h_t\mid Y_{0:t}) dh_t

The remaining useful life is:

\mathrm{RUL}_i = \mathbb{E}\left[T_i^{\text{failure}}-t\mid Y_{0:t}\right]

D.4 Loop 4: Model Adaptation

Timescale: Days to months.

Dynamics: \text{Data}\rightarrow\xi_{t+1}

Formalization: The model parameters \xi_t\in\Xi are updated via Bayesian inference:

\xi_{t+1} \sim P(\xi_t\mid Y_{0:t+1})

Connection to Digital Twin: The digital twin provides the synchronization infrastructure for model adaptation.

D.5 Loop 5: Structural Reconfiguration

Timescale: Weeks to years.

Dynamics: G_t\rightarrow G_{t+1}

Formalization: The topology G_t=(V_t,E_t) evolves according to the reconfiguration operator:

G_{t+1} = \Gamma(G_t,z_t,u_t)

Admissibility: \Gamma\in\mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}} as defined in Assumption A6.

D.6 Timescale Separation

Assumption A5 (restated). The five loops admit a strict timescale hierarchy with explicit small parameters:

\epsilon_1=\frac{\tau_P}{\tau_O},\quad
\epsilon_2=\frac{\tau_O}{\tau_H},\quad
\epsilon_3=\frac{\tau_H}{\tau_M},\quad
\epsilon_4=\frac{\tau_M}{\tau_R}

with \epsilon_i\in(0,\epsilon_{\max}) and \epsilon_{\max}\ll 1.

Theorem D.1 (Tikhonov). Under these conditions, the coupled system admits a standard singular perturbation form, and the boundary-layer systems are exponentially stable uniformly in the slow variables. The reduced-order approximation is valid.

Reference: Singular perturbation methods in the synthesis of control policies for multi-stage production-inventory systems.

