Appendix G: Sim-to-Real Validation Roadmap

G.1 The Sim-to-Real Gap

Problem Statement: "The sim-to-real transfer gap continue[s] to hinder the widespread industrial application of RL". RL "is still limited to industrial applications and suffers from three major challenges: sample inefficiency, real data collection, and the gap between simulator and reality".

Sources of the Gap:

· Dynamic modeling errors
· Sensor noise
· Differences in friction models between simulation and reality
· Visual discrepancies (lighting, texture)
· Contact dynamics mismatches
· Force/torque measurement discrepancies

G.2 Proposed Validation Protocol

Phase 1: Digital Model Playground

· Coating scenario simulation
· Domain randomization for robustness
· Policy training in simulation

Phase 2: Hardware-in-the-Loop

· Physical integration testing
· Real-time constraint verification
· Safety validation

Phase 3: Continuous Operation Testing

· 1000-hour continuous operation
· Sim-to-real quantification
· Performance degradation analysis

Remark G.1. No specific standard for 1000-hour autonomous operation was found in the literature. This remains a proposed validation protocol rather than an established metric. However, recent industrial milestones demonstrate feasibility: Natura Resources achieved 1,000 hours of autonomous operation on its Molten Salt Test System, with more than 750 hours monitored remotely by system operators. Similarly, Blue Water Autonomy surpassed 1000 hours of testing on its ship-scale autonomous test vessel.

G.3 Quantification Metrics

Sim-to-Real Drop:

\Delta_{\text{sim-real}} = \frac{J_{\text{sim}}(\pi) - J_{\text{real}}(\pi)}{J_{\text{sim}}(\pi)}

where J_{\text{sim}} and J_{\text{real}} are the expected returns in simulation and reality, respectively.

Recent Results: PACE attains an average real-world success rate of 93.3% and only a 2.7-percentage-point sim-to-real drop on contact-rich assembly tasks.

Acceptance Criteria:

· \Delta_{\text{sim-real}} < 10\%
· No safety violations during 1000-hour operation
· Performance degradation < 5% over the test duration

